      "problem": "Evaluate the integral ${\\int }_{-1/3}^{0}9x\\sqrt{3x+1}\\,\\text{d}x.$",
      "worked_solution": "Let $u=3x+1.$ Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=3\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{3}\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nWe also note that $x=\\frac{1}{3}(u-1).$ \n\nComputing our limits of integration, we get\n\n| **$x$** | $-1/3$ | $0$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| **$u$** | $0$ | $1$ |\n\nWe can now solve the integral using our new variable $u,$ remembering to use the new limits of integration:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}9x\\sqrt{3x+1}\\,\\text{d}x & =9{\\int }_{-1/3}^{0}\\frac{u-1}{3}\\cdot \\sqrt{u}\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}({u}^{3/2}-{u}^{1/2})\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={(\\frac{1}{5/2}{u}^{5/2}-\\frac{1}{3/2}{u}^{3/2})|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & ={(\\frac{2}{5}{u}^{5/2}-\\frac{2}{3}{u}^{3/2})|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =(\\frac{2}{5}\\cdot {1}^{5/2}-\\frac{2}{3}\\cdot {1}^{3/2})-(\\frac{2}{5}\\cdot {0}^{5/2}-\\frac{2}{3}\\cdot {0}^{3/2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{5}-\\frac{2}{3} \\\\  & =-\\frac{4}{15}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
