      "name": null,
      "problem": "Evaluate the integral ${\\int }_{0}^{1}x{(1+x)}^{4}\\,\\text{d}x.$",
      "worked_solution": "Let $u=1+x.$ Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=1\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nWe also note that $x=u-1.$\n\nComputing our limits of integration, we get\n\n| **$x$** | $0$ | $1$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| **$u$** | $1$ | $2$ |\n\nWe can now solve the integral using our new variable $u,$ remembering to use the new limits of integration:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}x{(1+x)}^{4}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}(u-1){u}^{4}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}({u}^{5}-{u}^{4})\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={(\\frac{1}{6}{u}^{6}-\\frac{1}{5}{u}^{5})|}_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}({2}^{6}-{1}^{6})-\\frac{1}{5}({2}^{5}-{1}^{5}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}(64-1)-\\frac{1}{5}(32-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{63}{6}-\\frac{31}{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{21}{2}-\\frac{31}{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{43}{10}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
